<p>After the Nigeys died, {% person "frank-wetzstein" %}Frank Wetzstein{% endperson %} and former Morton County sheriff {% person "charles-mcdonald" %}Charles McDonald{% endperson %} purchased the hotel in 1929 and operated it as the McDonald Hotel. {% person "fred-sommerfield" %}Fred Sommerfield{% endperson %} bought McDonald's interest after McDonald's death. Wetzstein owned and operated the adjacent {% place "palace-theatre" %}Palace Theatre{% endplace %}.</p>

<p>Fire destroyed the hotel in April 1967. Patrons of the {% place "corner-bar-hotel-nigey" %}Corner Bar{% endplace %} in the building's northwest basement first reported the blaze around 10 p.m., while the city's annual Firemen's Ball was underway at the {% place "mandan-elks-club" %}Elks Club{% endplace %} one block away. {% place "northwestern-bell" %}Northwestern Bell{% endplace %} had already moved the telephone exchange to a larger building across the street.</p>

<p>The building's tenants at the time of the fire included the {% place "greyhound-bus-depot-hotel-nigey" %}Greyhound bus depot{% endplace %}, {% place "baumstarck-pfaff-sewing-center" %}Baumstarck Pfaff Sewing Center{% endplace %}, {% place "mandan-credit-bureau" %}Mandan Credit Bureau{% endplace %}, and the offices of optometrists {% person "a-l-larson" %}A. L. Larson{% endperson %} and {% person "michael-riisager" %}Michael Riisager{% endperson %}, as well as the hotel's 54 rooms on the upper floors. The ruins were cleared for a parking lot that later served the Mandan branch of {% place "wells-fargo-bank" %}Wells Fargo Bank{% endplace %}.</p>

